{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>My spirit shall not judge in man.</b> Rashi: My spirit shall not become angered and contend with me on account of man. We find a parallel in Job, concerning whom HaShem said to the Adversary: 'There is none like him on earth, a blameless man' etc. Another interpretation: the language of נדן and תיק (sheath, pouch), that is, my spirit shall not be placed in its sheath — this is the body of man." "<b>For an extended time.</b> For a long time, as in 'and he sat there לעולם' and 'they shall serve לעולם'; meaning, the creatures alive now shall not endure for a long time." "<b>Inasmuch as he is flesh.</b> Because he too is flesh and is not a thing that endures forever, therefore his days should be shortened, and they shall not live as they have lived until now — for through this they corrupt their ways, robbing and plundering, since they do not fear death." "<b>And his days shall be a hundred and twenty years.</b> Rashi: there is no earlier and later etc. And one should not raise the objection: but it is said in Pesaḥim (6b) that in a single matter, what is earlier is earlier and what is later is later? For this must be two distinct matters, since it is established that from the birth of Yefet until the Flood was only a hundred years; and because of the honor of Noaḥ — so that people should not say: how did Noaḥ beget in haste after the consolation? — Scripture placed 'and Noaḥ begot Shem' before 'and He relented that He had made man.' Similar to this is what Rashi says at the end of Parashat Lekh Lekha."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:12.268865756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}